  "discussions": [
    {
      "item": "5A1: White Knight Acres LLC Rezoning - Town of Wayne",
      "body": "Staff presenter Garrett Pogle reported the petition to rezone from General Agriculture to Rural Residential near Chesk Road and Aspen Drive. The Town of Wayne recommended approval and staff also recommended approval. No public testified. Supervisor Drebeck moved to approve based on findings of fact, conclusions of law, and town approval; Supervisor Sefelt seconded. Motion carried unanimously."
    },
    {
      "item": "5A2: Elizabeth Kurt and Doug and Barb Kurt Rezoning - Town of Huitt",
      "body": "Staff reported a rezoning of parcels near Mile Road and Sunrise Road, reorganizing boundaries between General Agriculture and Rural Residential designations. The Town of Huitt recommended approval and staff concurred. No public testified. Supervisor Shaneborn moved to approve; Supervisor Brew seconded. Motion carried unanimously."
    },
    {
      "item": "5A3: Donna Klein Tippy Rezoning - Town of Reed",
      "body": "Staff reported a petition to rezone from General Agriculture to Rural Residential at the corner of Martin Road and County Road J. The Town of Reed recommended approval and staff concurred. No public testified. Supervisor Sefelt moved to approve and forward to the county board; Supervisor Enson seconded. Motion carried unanimously."
    },
    {
      "item": "5A4: Dwayne Johnson Rezoning - Town of Hull",
      "body": "Staff reported a petition to rezone approximately 8 acres from Low Density Residential and Farmland Preservation to Rural Estate west of Chestnut Hill Lane and Elderberry Road. Property owner Dwayne Johnson testified, explaining the land was a former cow yard with a waterway running through it, of little farming value, and he intended to sell it to a neighboring property owner. Town of Hull and staff recommended approval. Supervisor Sefelt moved to approve; Supervisor Shaneborn seconded. Motion carried unanimously."
    },
    {
      "item": "5A5: Thomas and Pamela Balls and Devon and Louisa Lammansky Rezoning - Town of Rib Falls",
      "body": "Staff reported a petition to rezone from General Agriculture to Low Density Residential south of Schoolhouse Lane and Countryside Drive. Town of Rib Falls and staff recommended approval. No public testified. Supervisor Brew moved to approve; Supervisor Enson seconded. Motion carried unanimously."
    },
    {
      "item": "5A6: Austin Leel Rezoning - Town of Holton",
      "body": "Staff reported a petition to rezone from General Agriculture to Rural Estate at the corner of Pope Road and County Line Road. Town of Holton and staff recommended approval. No public testified. Supervisor Shaneborn moved to approve; Supervisor Brew seconded. Motion carried unanimously."
    },
    {
      "item": "5A7: Marvin Martin Rezoning - Town of Hull",
      "body": "Staff reported a petition to rezone from Farmland Preservation to Light Industrial west of the intersection of County Road N and Silver Maple Lane. Town of Hull and staff recommended approval. No public testified. Supervisor Drebeck moved to approve; Supervisor Shaneborn seconded. Motion carried unanimously."
    },
    {
      "item": "5A8: Jordan Reynolds Rezoning - Town of Hull",
      "body": "Staff noted the agenda incorrectly listed this as Farmland Preservation to Light Industrial; the correct and publicly noticed rezoning was Farmland Preservation to Rural Estate. Staff reported the petition for land south of the intersection of Choke Cherry Road and Dill Creek Lane. Town of Hull and staff recommended approval. No public testified. Supervisor Sefelt moved to approve and forward to the county board; Supervisor Drebeck seconded. Motion carried unanimously."
    },
    {
      "item": "5A9: Spencer Joint Revocable Trust Rezoning - Town of McMillan (POSTPONED)",
      "body": "Staff reported a petition to rezone from Farmland Preservation to General Agriculture at the intersection of Galvan Avenue and Weeping Willow Lane, but noted the Town of McMillan had not submitted a required resolution. Staff recommended postponement until the town submits its resolution. The committee agreed, with Supervisor [inaudible] moving to postpone and Supervisor Enson seconding. Motion to postpone carried unanimously."
    },
    {
      "item": "5C1: Acquisition of Land Adjacent to Cherokee Park",
      "body": "Parks and Recreation Director Jamie Paulie presented a proposal to acquire approximately 14 acres of wooded land adjacent to Cherokee Park from a private landowner at an assessed value of $4,450 per acre, funded from the county's Parkland Dedication Account which currently holds $344,043. Paulie clarified only the wooded parcel would be purchased; farmland would remain in private ownership. Supervisor Enson noted a discrepancy between the 14-acre figure and the appraisal's 19-acre figure; Paulie acknowledged this and explained the appraisal was for per-acre valuation only. Supervisor Drebeck asked about potential timber harvesting; Paulie said no assessment had been done but noted access could be through the park. The Parks, Forestry, and Recreation Committee had already recommended approval. Supervisor Sefelt moved to approve and forward to the HR Finance and Property Committee; Supervisor Brew seconded. Motion carried unanimously."
    },
    {
      "item": "5C2: Donation of Pontoon Boat to Parks Department",
      "body": "Director Paulie presented a proposal to accept donation of a 1990 19-foot aluminum pontoon boat with a 50-horsepower motor from a local school district that was divesting from its fishing program. The boat, valued at approximately $4,000, would expand the county's youth fishing program at no cost. Paulie noted it would be added to the county fleet for routine maintenance and could be sold if upkeep became excessive. Supervisor Sefelt quipped the next Parks, Forestry, and Recreation Committee meeting would be held on the pontoon boat. Supervisor Brew moved to accept the donation; Supervisor Enson seconded. Motion carried unanimously."
    },
    {
      "item": "5B1: Town of Reb Rock Wind Energy Ordinance",
      "body": "Land Resources Manager Shad Harvey explained a new procedural change: the county now only reviews independent town ordinances for procedural compliance rather than legal content, with towns assuming legal responsibility via a signed application. Corporation Counsel confirmed the county is not vouching for the legality of the ordinance language. Supervisor Leur expressed concern about the word 'hope' regarding liability and sought clarification; Corp Counsel acknowledged the procedure is untested in Marathon County and that while liability probability is low, it cannot be guaranteed at 0%. Supervisor Drebeck moved to accept the Town of Reb Rock Wind Energy Ordinance; Supervisor Sefelt seconded. Motion carried, but was noted as not unanimous."
    },
    {
      "item": "5B2: Town of Reb Rock Solar Energy Ordinance",
      "body": "Harvey presented the same procedural review framework for the solar energy ordinance. Supervisor Sefelt asked whether towns could sign an agreement holding the county harmless; Harvey confirmed the application form serves that function. Chair Gibbs asked whether Corp Counsel reviews ordinance language for legality; Corp Counsel confirmed they do not review the language itself, only procedural compliance. Supervisor Sefelt moved to accept; Supervisor Brew seconded. Motion carried, with at least one 'no' vote noted."
    },
    {
      "item": "5B3: Town of Wausau Data Center Ordinance",
      "body": "Harvey presented the Town of Wausau data center ordinance under the same procedural review. Chair Gibbs noted the application had only one supervisor's signature, no second supervisor, and the clerk had not dated it. A Town of Wausau supervisor present at the meeting explained that one supervisor position is currently vacant due to a resignation and that the clerk's auto-signature was pre-approved by staff. The town representative expressed frustration with the statutory requirement that independent town ordinances go through the county, arguing that if towns bear full legal responsibility they should have full authority. Corp Counsel acknowledged the procedural anomaly and agreed with the sentiment but noted it is required by state law. Supervisor Shaneborn moved to approve; Supervisor Drebeck seconded. Motion carried, with at least one 'no' vote noted."
    },
    {
      "item": "6A1: Initial Conversation on Data Center Ordinance Development",
      "body": "Shad Harvey opened a brainstorming discussion on what issues a future data center ordinance should address, noting the county is under a 12-month moratorium on data centers and has no comprehensive standards. Key concerns raised included accurate information on water and electricity use (Supervisor Brew cited conversation with an IT instructor who argued water use is far lower than perceived), indirect water use tied to energy generation (Supervisor Leur), zoning classification and scale thresholds such as distinguishing large-scale hyperscale facilities from school server rooms (Shad Harvey and Supervisor Drebeck), state authority versus local authority, cumulative impacts of stacked projects, noise, decommissioning, and backup generator emissions. Chair Robinson noted WCA has two model ordinances from the Tulsa law firm and suggested a data center notification ordinance to ensure county awareness of projects in incorporated municipalities. Supervisor Brew asked if there had been any data center inquiries; Garrett Pogle confirmed one developer inquiry approximately a year to a year and a half ago. The committee directed staff to continue research, seek independent expert speakers, and examine what other communities have done, with no ordinance drafting yet to occur."
    },
